    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90825.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 33,
    "turn_text": "Well, President Trump is trying to crack down on this. He tried to, he should have done it two years ago. You see the degree to which to make these changes statutory are very difficult. You heard a lot of the Democrats saying, what do you mean you can't come here and be on welfare? Where do you against poor people? I'm not against poor people, but poor people should come here and work. I assume most of our ancestors didn't have a lot of money the day they got here, but they began to work or had somebody sponsor them. By the way, a huge thing that we should focus on is why people are coming here. And this frustrates the border patrol. There was times in the last 25 years where you had, were 30 years in which you had civil wars in Nicaragua, civil wars in Guatemala. Real bad things. People weren't coming to the United States. Why? Because they knew we had an immigration law that was going to be enforced. It frustrates the border patrol that now that things are better than they were in 30 or 35 years ago in Guatemala, or better than they were in Nicaragua, that now is the time people are coming here. Why? Because they can. And the number of people crossing our border goes up and down as to whether the word is out worldwide that the U.S. is enforcing the law. I was told by a Border Patrol agent they were bored when Donald Trump got elected. Why? Because Donald Trump claimed he was going to do something about the immigration. So people said, oh, the United States I'm not going to try to sneak across the border. Then it turns out he doesn't do so much. People come across. Then the rumors he's going to build a wall. Oh, the United States is serious again. Things go down. They reach the peak of over 150,000 in May. I believe about that amount. Now, since then they've gone down because Donald Trump threatened Mexico with tariffs. Some Mexican military are on the southern border or on the Guatemalan border, things drop. But some people are still coming across and our border patrol believes that is because the Mexican military sometimes turns a blind eye when it comes to dealing with the cartels. I had heard that even prior to President Trump's announcement of possible tariffs against Mexico. Why? Well, we were told that along the Arizona border, if you saw a Mexican military vehicle pass, It would not be unusual within a couple hours to have a drug shipment coming across. In other words, the board of patrol, not certain but just suspected that the Mexican military might be acting in cahoots with the board of patrol. And they feel that same way again, that even though we're getting less immigrants than it may, maybe the reason we're still getting the immigrants we are is because the drug cartels are so powerful that sometimes the Mexican military doesn't stand up to them.",
